{
  "title": "Before you make this load-bearing: four measured constraints on the self-hosted deployment",
  "purpose": "Confirm that everyone who can deploy, scale, or front this service with a proxy has read the four constraints that will silently break it, and capture the decisions we still owe: who owns the replica pin, what the proxy timeout actually is here, and whether participant links are acceptable as attribution for our use. Answers go into docs/SELF-HOSTING.md as an owners section and into the runbook before we hand the service to the team.",
  "resumeHint": "Once answers are in: update docs/SELF-HOSTING.md with named owners, set MAX_AWAIT_MS for our proxy, and add the replica-pin check to the deploy checklist. Then deploy.",
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Deploy owner",
      "role": "Runs the deploy and owns the Deployment manifest"
    },
    {
      "name": "Platform / on-call",
      "role": "Owns the proxy, CDN, and whatever answers a 524 at 3am"
    },
    {
      "name": "Service owner",
      "role": "Accountable for what the service promises about identity and attribution"
    }
  ],
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  },
  "visibility": "open",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Read this before the deploy, not after the incident",
        "lead": "Four constraints govern this service. None of them were designed in — each was found in production, and each fails quietly rather than loudly. Three of the four look like everything is working while the guarantee underneath is gone: replay protection that has stopped protecting, agents installing a client four hours stale, callers unable to distinguish a dead wait from an empty one.",
        "points": [
          "Exactly one replica. Horizontal scaling does not degrade replay protection, it disables it.",
          "Long polls must return inside the proxy's timeout. Ours is ~100s at Cloudflare; MAX_AWAIT_MS defaults to 90s.",
          "A CDN may cache by file extension whatever your origin headers say. The canonical client URL is /mcp, no extension.",
          "A participant link is a capability, not an identity. Forwarding the link forwards the ability to answer as that person."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The settings these constraints reduce to",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "replicas",
            "value": "1, with strategy Recreate. Not a performance choice — a correctness one."
          },
          {
            "key": "Nonce store",
            "value": "In memory (src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts). Per-process, not shared."
          },
          {
            "key": "Freshness window",
            "value": "5 minutes. A captured request is replayable inside it if the nonce is unknown to the receiving process."
          },
          {
            "key": "MAX_AWAIT_MS",
            "value": "90000 by default. Must sit below whatever proxy fronts the service."
          },
          {
            "key": "Cloudflare origin timeout",
            "value": "~100s, then a 524 with no body. Measured: a 150s poll died at 125s."
          },
          {
            "key": "Canonical client URL",
            "value": "/mcp — /mcp.js remains only as an alias for saved URLs."
          },
          {
            "key": "LINK_TTL_HOURS",
            "value": "Bounds how long a participant link stays answerable. Expiry is checked in the resolving query, not after it."
          },
          {
            "key": "Revocation",
            "value": "revoked_at exists in the schema and is honoured on every resolve, but is not exposed over HTTP. Killing a link today is a manual UPDATE on participant_links."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The four constraints, in the order they will bite you",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "Run exactly one replica",
            "detail": "Every signed request carries a nonce accepted once; that is what stops a captured request being replayed inside its five-minute freshness window. The nonce store is in process memory. With two replicas, a nonce burned on one is unknown to the other, and a replayed request routed to the second one succeeds. This is the single most important operational constraint here. Scaling out is possible — it means moving the nonce store to Postgres or Redis first. The moment replicas changes, that has to change with it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Long polls die at about 100 seconds behind Cloudflare",
            "detail": "Cloudflare gives an origin roughly 100 seconds before it answers 524 itself. Measured through the tunnel: a 150-second poll died at 125 seconds with HTTP 524 and no body. Not a slow response — no response, and no way for the caller to tell a dead wait from an empty one. Holding a connection past that ceiling does not make the caller wait longer, it makes the wait fail. MAX_AWAIT_MS therefore defaults to 90 seconds: every poll returns inside the window, and one that ran out its budget returns timedOut: true with whatever has arrived so far. A timeout is a legitimate outcome, not an error — the MCP client loops shorter polls, so a caller asking for five minutes gets five minutes. Behind a different proxy, find its timeout and set MAX_AWAIT_MS below it. Whatever sits in front of this service decides the real ceiling; the service only decides whether it respects it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A CDN may cache by file extension regardless of what you send",
            "detail": "/mcp.js was served with cache-control: no-store and came back from the edge with cf-cache-status: HIT and an injected max-age=14400. Cloudflare caches .js by default, by extension, whatever the origin intended. For four hours after every deploy, agents installed the previous client. The fix is in the path, not the headers: the canonical URL is /mcp, with no extension. If you put any CDN in front of this, check what it caches. Origin headers are a request, not an instruction, and the failure is silent — everything works, one deploy behind."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Participant links are capabilities",
            "detail": "Anyone holding a participant link can answer as that participant; a forwarded link is a forwarded identity. This is deliberate and it is why the product works at all: participants need no account, no email verification, and no Claude access. Attribution is whichever link was used — which costs nothing and is honest about what it is. It is not an audit trail. Do not deploy expecting one. If responses ever need to be contested, that is the point to add verified identity, and that is a change to this service, not a configuration option waiting to be switched on."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "How each one fails, and what you would actually see",
        "columns": [
          "Constraint",
          "Failure mode",
          "What you observe",
          "Correct response"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "replicas: 1",
            "Replay protection off",
            "Nothing. All requests succeed, including replayed ones.",
            "Pin replicas: 1 / Recreate; move the nonce store before ever changing it"
          ],
          [
            "MAX_AWAIT_MS < proxy timeout",
            "Poll killed mid-wait",
            "HTTP 524, empty body, caller cannot tell dead from empty",
            "Measure the proxy in front of you; set MAX_AWAIT_MS below it"
          ],
          [
            "CDN extension caching",
            "Stale client served",
            "Nothing. Agents run the previous deploy for up to four hours.",
            "Serve /mcp; verify cf-cache-status on the canonical path after deploy"
          ],
          [
            "Link-as-capability",
            "Attribution misread as proof",
            "An answer attributed to someone who never opened the link",
            "State the limit; add verified identity if answers must be contestable"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "Three of the four are silent. That is why this is a pre-deploy read rather than a runbook entry."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The three checks worth running against the deployment before it carries traffic",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "Replica count and strategy. Anything other than 1 / Recreate is a correctness bug, not a capacity setting.",
            "language": "bash",
            "code": "kubectl get deploy <name> -o jsonpath='{.spec.replicas} {.spec.strategy.type}{\"\\n\"}'\n# expected: 1 Recreate"
          },
          {
            "note": "Edge caching on the canonical client path. A HIT with an injected max-age means agents will install a stale client.",
            "language": "bash",
            "code": "curl -sSI https://<host>/mcp | grep -Ei 'cf-cache-status|cache-control|age'\n# expected: no HIT, no injected max-age"
          },
          {
            "note": "Await ceiling against the real proxy. If this returns 524 rather than timedOut: true, MAX_AWAIT_MS is above the proxy's limit.",
            "language": "bash",
            "code": "echo \"MAX_AWAIT_MS=$MAX_AWAIT_MS\"  # must be below the front proxy's origin timeout\n# Cloudflare: ~100s -> 90000 is safe"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "The questions below are the point of this page. They are short, and they are what turns four constraints written down into four constraints someone owns."
    },
    {
      "widget": "approval",
      "props": {
        "title": "Deploy with these constraints as written",
        "proposal": "Deploy the service with replicas: 1 / Recreate, MAX_AWAIT_MS at or below the front proxy's origin timeout, /mcp as the canonical client path, and participant links documented as attribution rather than audit.",
        "detail": "Approving means you accept that scaling out is a code change (moving the nonce store to Postgres or Redis), not a manifest edit, and that responses collected through this service are not evidence in a dispute. Reject or ask for changes if any of those is unacceptable for the workload you are about to put on it."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "What sits in front of the service in our deployment?",
        "context": "Whatever fronts this service decides the real long-poll ceiling and the real caching behaviour. The service only decides whether it respects them.",
        "question": "Which proxy or edge is in the request path?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "cloudflare-tunnel",
            "label": "Cloudflare tunnel — ~100s ceiling, .js cached by extension"
          },
          {
            "value": "cloudflare-proxy",
            "label": "Cloudflare proxied DNS (orange cloud), no tunnel"
          },
          {
            "value": "nginx-ingress",
            "label": "nginx / ingress controller only, no CDN"
          },
          {
            "value": "cloud-lb",
            "label": "Cloud load balancer (ALB / GCLB / similar)"
          },
          {
            "value": "other",
            "label": "Something else — I will name it and its timeout below"
          },
          {
            "value": "unknown",
            "label": "I do not know yet — this needs measuring before deploy"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "The four things that need an owner",
        "intro": "Short answers are fine. Blank answers are how the next person inherits an undocumented constraint.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "proxy_timeout",
            "label": "What is the measured origin timeout of the proxy you selected above, in seconds? Say 'unmeasured' if it has not been tested with a real long poll.",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "max_await_ms",
            "label": "What value should MAX_AWAIT_MS be set to for this deployment, in milliseconds?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "replica_owner",
            "label": "Who owns the replicas: 1 pin — i.e. who must be asked before anyone scales this, and where is that recorded?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "cdn_check",
            "label": "How will we verify after each deploy that /mcp is not being cached at the edge? Name the check and where it runs.",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "revocation_plan",
            "label": "Revoking a link today is a manual UPDATE on participant_links. Who can run it, and what is the LINK_TTL_HOURS value we should ship with?",
            "required": true
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "rate-items",
      "props": {
        "title": "How confident are you that we would catch each of these in production?",
        "items": [
          {
            "id": "replica_scale_out",
            "label": "Someone scales to 2 replicas and replay protection silently stops"
          },
          {
            "id": "poll_524",
            "label": "A long poll is killed by the proxy and returns 524 with no body"
          },
          {
            "id": "stale_client",
            "label": "Agents install a four-hour-stale MCP client after a deploy"
          },
          {
            "id": "forwarded_link",
            "label": "A participant link is forwarded and someone answers as someone else"
          }
        ],
        "scale": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "ask",
      "props": {
        "title": "The thing this page did not cover",
        "context": "Each of these four was found rather than anticipated. The list is almost certainly not finished.",
        "field": {
          "kind": "textarea",
          "id": "blockers",
          "label": "What would stop you deploying this, or what constraint do you expect we have not found yet?",
          "required": false
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Three self-hosting bugs whose symptoms pointed somewhere other than their cause",
  "purpose": "A reference write-up for anyone self-hosting this project, so the next person who sees `unauthorized` on every call, a mangled `claude mcp add` line, or a silently ignored environment variable spends their time on the actual cause instead of rediscovering the diagnosis. Nothing here is being asked; this page collects no answers.",
  "resumeHint": "If you are hitting one of these on a fresh deployment, check the three items under 'What to verify on your own deployment' before reading any further into signatures, transports, or auth.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Three things self-hosting exposed",
        "lead": "Running the project somewhere other than the original host surfaced three defects that had been invisible on the deployment that wrote them. Each announced itself with a symptom that pointed confidently at the wrong subsystem: an auth failure that was really an address failure, a shell command that was really a template-literal escape, and a config default that was really a parser giving up in silence. All three are fixed. The write-up is here because the misdirection is the expensive part, not the fix.",
        "points": [
          "`unauthorized` on every call was the client talking to the wrong host, not signing badly.",
          "A joined-up `claude mcp add` command was a line continuation eaten by a template literal, not a broken renderer.",
          "MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s becoming 90 seconds was an unparseable value falling back to a default without saying so.",
          "A fourth item — a CSS class collision — got a test rather than a fix, because it had already been found by eye twice."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "Symptom, where it points, where it actually was",
        "columns": [
          "Symptom",
          "What it reads as",
          "Actual cause"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "`unauthorized` on every MCP call from a freshly installed client",
            "A broken or mismatched signature; key handling; clock skew",
            "The client had this deployment's URL hardcoded as its default base URL, so it signed correct requests and sent them to somebody else's host, which had never seen the key"
          ],
          [
            "The `claude mcp add` command on /start arrives as one long joined line",
            "A markdown or code-block renderer dropping newlines",
            "A lone trailing backslash inside a template literal is a line continuation, not a backslash — the source lines were joined and the continuation character silently eaten before rendering ever happened"
          ],
          [
            "MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s behaves as 90 seconds; awaits time out early or late",
            "A unit-parsing feature that exists and is being applied",
            "The value failed to parse as a number and the loader fell back to its default without reporting it, in a loader whose whole stated posture is refusing loudly"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "In each row, the symptom was truthful and the inference from it was wrong. That is the pattern worth remembering more than any of the three fixes."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The three, in detail",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The MCP client hardcoded one deployment as its default base URL",
            "detail": "A self-hoster who followed /start by hand installed a client whose default base URL was this deployment, not theirs. The client then signed requests with their key and sent them to a host that had never issued that key. Every call returned `unauthorized`, which reads as a signature problem, so the natural next move is to inspect key derivation, canonicalisation and clock skew — all of which are fine. The address was wrong. Fixed by having the instance substitute its own URL into the client file as it serves it, the way /cli already worked: a client downloaded from your deployment now talks to your deployment."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A trailing backslash inside a template literal is a line continuation",
            "detail": "The multi-line `claude mcp add` command on /start was written in a JavaScript template literal with shell line continuations at the end of each line. In a template literal, a lone trailing backslash continues the source line: the newline and the backslash both disappear before the string exists. The rendered page therefore showed one joined line, with no trace of the continuation, and the page renderer — the obvious suspect — was innocent. Escape the backslash (`\\\\`) or build the command from an array and join it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "An unparseable numeric env var fell back to its default in silence",
            "detail": "`MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s` looks like it means ninety seconds. It parsed to nothing, the loader substituted the default, and the process started normally. The behaviour that follows is a timeout that is neither what you configured nor obviously wrong — the worst kind, because the config file and the running system disagree with no evidence anywhere. This is in a loader whose stated posture is to refuse loudly rather than guess. It refuses now: an unparseable numeric value is a startup failure, not a shrug."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Fourth item: a stylesheet collision, fixed with a test rather than an eye",
            "detail": "The gallery page defined `.label`, which is also what the shared block renderer calls a field label. Every input inside a preview rendered uppercase and tiny. This class of bug has bitten twice and both times was caught by somebody noticing it, which is not a method. There is now a test that no page stylesheet redefines a class the shared stylesheet defines. It was proved to fail against the collision before being trusted — a guard that has never been seen to bite is an assertion about a guard, not a guard."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two that are easiest to reproduce",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The continuation bug. Inside a template literal, the backslash at the end of each line is consumed by JavaScript, not passed through to the reader as shell syntax. What the page shows is one line, and the backslashes are gone.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "// Wrong: the newlines and backslashes vanish before the string exists\nconst cmd = `claude mcp add sirdocalot \\\n  --transport http \\\n  ${baseUrl}/mcp`;\n\n// Renders as:\n//   claude mcp add sirdocalot   --transport http   https://example/mcp\n\n// Right: escape the backslash, or don't use one\nconst cmd = [\n  'claude mcp add sirdocalot \\\\',\n  '  --transport http \\\\',\n  `  ${baseUrl}/mcp`,\n].join('\\n');"
          },
          {
            "note": "The silent-default bug. The old shape returns the default for any value that fails to parse, including values that were plainly meant to configure something. The new shape distinguishes 'absent' from 'present and nonsense'.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "// Before: MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s -> NaN -> default, silently\nconst n = Number(process.env[key]);\nreturn Number.isFinite(n) ? n : fallback;\n\n// After: absent is fine, unparseable is fatal\nconst raw = process.env[key];\nif (raw === undefined || raw === '') return fallback;\nconst n = Number(raw);\nif (!Number.isFinite(n)) {\n  throw new Error(`${key}=${raw} is not a number (expected milliseconds)`);\n}\nreturn n;"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "What to verify on your own deployment",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "Client base URL",
            "value": "Open the MCP client file your instance serves and confirm the default base URL is your host, not the one you copied from. If it isn't, you downloaded a pre-fix client — fetch it again from your own deployment."
          },
          {
            "key": "The /start command",
            "value": "The `claude mcp add` block should appear as multiple lines with visible trailing backslashes. One long line means you are on a pre-fix build; the command will still work if you paste it, but read it before trusting it."
          },
          {
            "key": "Numeric environment variables",
            "value": "Values are milliseconds and must parse as bare numbers. `90s`, `1_000` and `90 ms` are now startup failures rather than silent defaults. If your instance suddenly refuses to boot after an upgrade, this is the likely reason and the error names the variable."
          },
          {
            "key": "Page stylesheets",
            "value": "Do not define a class the shared stylesheet already defines — `.label` in particular. The test suite now fails on collisions rather than waiting for someone to notice tiny uppercase text."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "text": "The common thread: all three defects were invisible on the deployment that authored them, because that deployment was the hardcoded default, rendered its own /start page, and had a valid config file. Self-hosting is not only a feature here; it is the test environment that makes assumptions about 'the host' fail loudly enough to find."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Three open questions in DESIGN.md: settle the core bet, the per-dev cluster, and MCP",
  "purpose": "Record where Priya and Sam each stand on the three items still marked OPEN in docs/DESIGN.md, with reasoning, so the OPEN markers can be resolved into stated decisions (or into a named piece of work that must happen before they can be). Where the two disagree, the disagreement is what gets written down.",
  "resumeHint": "Rewrite the three `OPEN` blocks in docs/DESIGN.md as decisions, quoting each maintainer's reasoning. If item 1 comes back as 'verify first', open that measurement task before touching anything else.",
  "visibility": "open",
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  },
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Priya",
      "role": "Maintainer — must sign off before the OPEN markers come out"
    },
    {
      "name": "Sam",
      "role": "Maintainer — must sign off before the OPEN markers come out"
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Where the argument stands",
        "lead": "DESIGN.md commits to one bet and leaves three things unresolved. The bet: the agent-facing surface is a schema an agent emits against, not a framework it programs against, because generating bespoke HTML costs thousands of output tokens every time and that generation — not context loading, not deploy latency — is where the current approach is slow and expensive. A schema emission is claimed at a 20–50× token reduction over an authored page, and that reduction is stated as the entire reason the service exists.",
        "points": [
          "The bet's premise is asserted, not measured. DESIGN.md itself says it is worth confirming against two or three real past generations before committing — and that if context loading turns out to dominate, the right answer is a cached design doc, not this service.",
          "Any design decision that pushes agents back toward writing markup breaks the bet. That is the standing test the other two items get judged against.",
          "The deployment shape is per-dev: each dev runs an independent single-node k3s cluster behind their own tunnel, so a participant link points at that dev's box — a box that is also a NixOS host that gets rebuilt.",
          "The insurance already taken is cheap and unarguable: never bake the host into a brief's identity. A handle plus a participant token identify a brief; the hostname is deployment configuration. That keeps 'move to one shared deployment' a config change rather than a migration.",
          "The REST API is built, bearer-authenticated, and is what the participant-facing app uses. An MCP server over the top — list_widgets, create_brief, await_responses — is proposed but not built. REST stays underneath either way, because an orchestrator resuming a session is not necessarily an agent."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Open item 1 — the core bet rests on an unmeasured premise"
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two sides of it",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "For proceeding now",
            "detail": "The 20–50× claim is large enough that even a badly wrong estimate leaves the conclusion intact. Schema-over-framework is also defensible on grounds the token argument does not depend on: consistency of output, a reviewable widget vocabulary, and no markup for an agent to get wrong."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "For measuring first",
            "detail": "DESIGN.md names generation as the dominant cost and then names the alternative hypothesis in the same breath: if context loading dominates instead, the correct artefact is a cached design doc and this service is the wrong shape entirely. That is not a rounding error in the estimate — it is a different project. Two or three real past generations would settle it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The cost of getting it wrong late",
            "detail": "Every decision downstream — the widget vocabulary, the MCP surface, the participant app — is justified by this premise. If it falls, they fall with it, and they fall after they are built."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 1 — your position",
        "context": "The measurement DESIGN.md asks for is: take two or three real past generations, and attribute their cost between generation and context loading.",
        "question": "What should happen to the `OPEN` marker on the core bet?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "measure-first",
            "label": "Measure first — block further work on the schema surface until two or three past generations have been attributed"
          },
          {
            "value": "measure-parallel",
            "label": "Measure in parallel — keep building, but treat the bet as provisional and run the attribution this cycle"
          },
          {
            "value": "accept",
            "label": "Accept the bet as stated — remove the OPEN marker, on the strength of the estimate plus the non-token arguments for a schema"
          },
          {
            "value": "reframe",
            "label": "I already believe context loading dominates — reframe toward a cached design doc"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Open item 2 — per-dev clusters and the dead link"
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The shape of the problem",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "Topology",
            "value": "One independent single-node k3s cluster per dev, each behind that dev's own tunnel"
          },
          {
            "key": "What a link points at",
            "value": "That dev's box, specifically"
          },
          {
            "key": "Failure mode",
            "value": "A stakeholder clicks six hours later and gets an answer only if the box is still up — and the box is a NixOS host that gets rebuilt"
          },
          {
            "key": "Blast radius today",
            "value": "One dev's briefs"
          },
          {
            "key": "Mitigation already taken",
            "value": "Host is never part of a brief's identity — handle plus participant token identify it; hostname is deployment config"
